// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #6437 Add Linux rule: Potential Shell Or Command Execution Via GDB
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6437
// Pull request author: defamp
// Change: new rule
// Title: Potential Shell Or Command Execution Via GDB
// Sigma ID: f73b3f35-82e7-492d-ad80-4e5ce268d5e0
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Defa Mulya Pratama
// Date: 2026-10-07 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, T1059
// Description: Detects the use of the GNU Debugger (gdb) to spawn a shell or execute operating system commands.
//   gdb can run arbitrary commands via its "shell" command, the "!" escape, an inline Python one-
//   liner, or "call system()". When gdb is executed with elevated privileges (e.g. via sudo), this
//   is abused for privilege escalation or to break out of restricted environments.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate debugging sessions that invoke the "shell" command may occur, but are uncommon in automation and should be reviewed.
// References:
//   - https://gtfobins.github.io/gtfobins/gdb/
//   - https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/security/current/linux-restricted-shell-breakout-via-linux-binary-s.html
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/9e4291ae411e383ddf23392ada2cd5fe40f86c64/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_gdb_shell_execution.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/gdb" and (ProcessCommandLine contains "shell " or ProcessCommandLine contains "!/bin/" or ProcessCommandLine contains "!sh" or ProcessCommandLine contains "!bash" or ProcessCommandLine contains "os.system" or ProcessCommandLine contains "call system" or ProcessCommandLine contains "call (void)system")
